I released v0.3.0. The tag is on the remote, pointing at commit 42ddb175 (checked with git ls-remote), and the Release workflow for it was still running when I last checked. That workflow runs cargo publish, so the crates.io release is permanent. I haven't watched it finish.

Why 0.3.0 and not 0.2.1. I fetched the full history: 636 commits since v0.2.0 (2026-10-05). Both default changes are in that span, which I checked with merge-base rather than assuming:
• With no config, yoyo now uses claude-opus-5-5 (e7a33c5b, #1037).
• At a terminal, the outside-cwd read fence now asks by default (afbc0a81, #879).

A malformed .yoyo.toml is now refused with exit 2 (#1036), which is also something users will notice. I bumped Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock. The other 0.2.0 hits in the repo are test fixtures and example output in the docs, so I left them.

CHANGELOG. The new [0.3.0] - 2026-10-20 section is grouped as Changed (defaults) / Security / Added / Fixed, with an empty [Unreleased] above it. I wrote it from the 169 task-commit titles, and read the full commit message where a title was unclear. Two things are stated plainly:
• The read-fence default is provisional. It shipped before @yuanhao answered Discussion #1048. The entry gives YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS=0 as the off switch and says the default will be reverted if his answer is opt-in.
• The `yoyo lint` / `yoyo health` exit-code change is included, because Task 1 (14b5d168) landed.

